From a01ac5d100938cb6faad1e9e3e6763a1a3df5ef7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 13:44:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] publish: the struct-js OIDC gap is closed, so stop
 documenting it as open

The header stated that @voxgig/struct-js had never published over OIDC and
that a `target: javascript` dispatch "cannot succeed" until a publisher was
registered. Both were true when written, on 2026-09-03, and neither is true
now: the registration landed the next day and the package has published over
OIDC twice since.

npm records what happened. 0.1.4 (gitHead 224ce10, run 19, 2026-09-04) and
0.1.5 (gitHead f856ceb, 2026-09-05) both carry a `trustedPublisher` of
`github` with a SLSA provenance attestation; 0.1.2 remains the lone token
publish, under a personal account with no attestation. Left as it stood, the
paragraph told the next person to cut a release that the javascript target
was blocked, which would cost them a dispatch to disprove.

Replaced rather than deleted, because two things in it are still worth
knowing. npm registers a trusted publisher only against a package that
already exists, which is why a renamed port needs one bootstrap publish
before OIDC is even configurable, and why 0.1.2 can never gain provenance.
And a 404 on the PUT still means the registration rather than this file.

Added the trap this repository actually hit: the two npm names are
registered separately and carry different OIDC config ids, so @voxgig/struct
publishing cleanly says nothing about @voxgig/struct-js. That asymmetry is
what made the 2026-09-03 failure confusing at the time.

Comment-only. Every changed line is a comment, the six jobs and three
dispatch inputs are untouched, and the file still parses.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01RBcWm7ygX2tNhUtqtTJbAh
---
 .github/workflows/publish.yml | 27 +++++++++++++++++----------
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/.github/workflows/publish.yml b/.github/workflows/publish.yml
index 6a3b55a..96f19c0 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/publish.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/publish.yml
@@ -113,16 +113,23 @@
 # publishers are updated to match. Do NOT publish locally over a token: that
 # bypasses OIDC and its provenance attestation entirely.
 #
-# @voxgig/struct-js HAS NEVER PUBLISHED OVER OIDC. The port was renamed from
-# @voxgig/structjs, and npm registers a trusted publisher only against a
-# package that already exists, so 0.1.2 went out as one plain token publish:
-# it sits on npm under a person's name with no provenance attestation, while
-# @voxgig/struct@0.3.4 carries one. A `target: javascript` dispatch on
-# 2026-09-03 failed the OIDC exchange all the same, reported as exactly the
-# 404 above -- tests passed, the tarball packed, the PUT was rejected. Until
-# a publisher for @voxgig/struct-js is registered against THIS repository and
-# THIS filename on the package's npm settings page, that dispatch cannot
-# succeed. The registration is what to check, not the workflow.
+# BOTH NPM PACKAGES PUBLISH OVER OIDC, and @voxgig/struct-js was the last to
+# get there. The port was renamed from @voxgig/structjs, and npm registers a
+# trusted publisher only against a package that already EXISTS, so 0.1.2 went
+# out as one plain token publish to bootstrap the name: it sits on npm under a
+# person's account with no provenance attestation, and it stays that way,
+# because a release cannot add provenance to a version already published. A
+# `target: javascript` dispatch on 2026-09-03 then failed the exchange as
+# exactly the 404 above -- tests passed, the tarball packed, the PUT was
+# rejected -- because the publisher was still unregistered. Registering it
+# fixed precisely that: 0.1.4 published over OIDC on 2026-09-04 and 0.1.5 on
+# 2026-09-05. @voxgig/struct has carried an attestation since 0.3.0.
+#
+# SO A 404 ON THE PUT MEANS THE REGISTRATION, not this file. Check it on the
+# package's npm settings page, against THIS repository and THIS filename, and
+# check the RIGHT package: the two npm names are registered separately and
+# carry different OIDC config ids, so one of them publishing proves nothing
+# about the other. A `dry_run` cannot test either -- see DRY RUN above.
 
 name: publish
 
-- 
